  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\n   The previous solver uses a binary representation and local search, which might get stuck in local optima. Our approach will use a continuous representation with simulated annealing (SA) and local search across multiple resolutions, with a move designed to preserve the integral constraint (sum(h)=n_points/2). This better matches the smooth nature of the optimal solution and provides global exploration capabilities. Key improvements:\n\n   - Continuous representation allows smoother optimization and faster convergence\n   - Simulated annealing for global exploration, then local refinement\n   - Mutli-resolution search to move between different point densities\n   - A specialized move operator that preserves the integral constraint\n   - Polynomial cooling schedule for SA\n\n   We'll start with the default resolution (initial_h_values, 112 points) and try increasing resolutions (224, 336, 448 points) as time allows. The move is designed to change two values simultaneously while maintaining the necessary constraints, and the temperature schedule balances exploration and exploitation.\n\n   This addresses the limitation of the binary approach and is expected to find lower C5 values by starting from a better representation and using global search techniques.\n\n  2. Mathematical Specification:\n\n   We optimize a continuous function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] sampled at N points, with the constraint:\n     sum_{i=1}^N h_i = N/2\n\n   Objective:\n     minimize max_{k} [ sum_{i=1}^N h_i * (1-h_{i+k}) * dx ]\n\n   where dx = 2.0/N.\n\n   Representation:\n     1D array of N floats in [0,1] with sum(N/2)\n\n   Constraints:\n     0 <= h_i <= 1 for all i\n     sum(h) = N/2\n\n   The move in SA changes two indices i and j simultaneously:\n     h[i] = (h[i]+h[j])/2 + delta\n     h[j] = (h[i]+h[j])/2 - delta\n\n   where delta is drawn from a normal distribution (mean=0, std=0.01). This change preserves the average of the two values and the overall sum.\n\n   The initial solution is derived by interpolating the provided initial_h_values at the current resolution.\n\n  3. Implementation Procedure:\n\n   We implement a multi-resolution simulated annealing algorithm:\n\n   Pseudocode:\n     def run(seed, budget_s):\n         best_c5 = float('inf')\n         best_h = None\n         best_n = 112   # default resolution\n\n         # Evaluate initial candidate\n         for n in [112, 224, 336, 448]:\n             if time_used() > budget_s: break\n             if n != 112:   # For increased resolution\n                 h_initial = interpolate_initial(initial_h_values, n)\n                 h = normalize_continuous(h_initial, n)\n\n             h_candidate, c5_val = simulated_annealing(h, remaining_budget)\n             if compute_c5_with_local_search(h_candidate) < c5_val:   # Further refinement\n                 h_candidate = local_search(h_candidate, remaining_budget/3)\n\n             if c5_val < best_c5:\n                 best_c5 = c5_val\n                 best_h = h_candidate.copy()\n                 best_n = n\n\n         return best_h, best_c5, best_n\n\n   Key helper functions:\n     - normalize_continuous: ensures sum(h)=n/2 and bounds [0,1]\n     - interpolate_initial: linear interpolation between initial points\n     - compute_c5: uses np.correlate as before\n     - simulated_annealing: uses exponential cooling schedule\n     - local_search: gradient-free refinement in continuous space\n\n   The SA phase has these parameters:\n     T_start = 0.99, T_end = 0.001, alpha = 0.99 (cooling factor)\n     Time budget distributed across resolutions\n\n  4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\n\n   Core algorithm remains fixed for each resolution:\n\n     Resolution search order: [112, 224, 336, 448] (powers of 2)\n\n     SA Parameters:\n       Temperature evolution: T = T0 * (1 - t_total/max_time)^{0.8} (polynomial cooling)\n\n     Specialized move operator:\n        preserves integral condition\n        designed for continuous space\n        symmetric change to maintain bounds\n\n     Local search parameters:\n        Maximum 20% time budget for refinement per resolution\n        Tolerance 1e-4 for termination\n\n     Gradient-based methods are not used due to non-convex problem\n\n   We implement:\n\n        def sa_step(h_current, T):\n            # Two-element move preserving sum\n            i, j = random.sample(range(N), 2)\n            avg = (h_current[i] + h_current[j])/2\n            delta = np.random.normal(0, 0.01)\n            new_h = h_current.copy()\n            new_h[i] = max(min(avg + delta, 1.0), 0.0)\n            new_h[j] = 2*avg - new_h[i]  # preserves the average\n            return new_h\n\n   This move is more effective than binary flipping and is likely to find lower C5 values.\n\n  5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\n   Time estimates:\n\n     n=112: ~2.5ms/evaluation, SA does ~1000 evals -> 2.5s at full time\n     n=224: ~9ms/evaluation, SA does 700 evals -> 6.3s\n     n=336: ~25ms/evaluation -> 5s\n     n=448: ~50ms/evaluation -> 8s\n\n     Total: ~22s per resolution without local search\n\n   Budget distribution:\n\n     50% SA (2000 evaluations), 30% local search (1500 evaluations), 20% resolution change\n\n   The algorithm is robust to:\n\n     - Numerical instability: all calls clip values to [0,1]\n     - Resolution change: interpolation maintains constraints\n     - Early termination: best known solution carried forward\n\n   Total runtime estimation: 448 points SA takes ~9ms*1000 = 9s per run\n\n  6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\n   All candidates are validated:\n\n     - 0 <= h_i <= 1 (after each move)\n     - sum(h) = N/2 exactly (normalized)\n     - c5 computed by the provided np.correlate method\n\n   The returned solution and score use the highest-resolution candidate that improved the C5 value.\n\n   Normalization: uses iterative clipping and scaling to meet the sum constraint.\n\n   Fall-back procedure: \n        If an evaluation takes too long or produces NaN, restore the solution to the interpolated and normalized parent\n\n   Final output structure must match the harness:\n\n        (best_h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\n   The scoring uses dx * max(convolution) as the official measure.",
